    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Choose any number on the interior of the table.
          Describe in words what happens to the values as you move away from
          this number.
          Consider movement in multiple directions, up, down, left, right, and
          diagonally.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider a second number from the table.
          Do the same patterns apply to this number as the one you chose in part
          (a)?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Because our patterns remain consistent for any number chosen, we can
          generalize the rules in terms of a variable.
          Suppose that we choose one square from the interior of the table and
          replace the value with <m>n</m>.
          Write each of the values around this number in terms of <m>n</m>,
          using the patterns you described above.
        </p>

        <tabular left="minor" top="minor" halign="center">
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor">
              <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/>
            </cell>
            <cell right="minor">
              <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/>
            </cell>
            <cell right="minor">
              <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor">
              <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/>
            </cell>
            <cell right="minor">
              <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <m>n</m> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/>
            </cell>
            <cell right="minor">
              <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/>
            </cell>
          </row>

          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell right="minor">
              <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/>
            </cell>
            <cell right="minor">
              <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/>
            </cell>
            <cell right="minor">
              <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/> <nbsp/>
            </cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="exA-ACN">
    <title>Adding consecutive numbers</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="exA-CN"/> we looked at sums of consecutive integers.
        As we continue working, we may want to ask the question, can we write
        <m>134</m> as the sum of 4 consecutive integers?
        We can generalize this by referring to the first value with a variable
        name, say <m>x</m>.
        The next larger number would then be <m>x+1</m>.
        The third and fourth numbers would be <m>x+2</m> and <m>x+3</m>
        respectively.
        Our question, can then be expressed as
        <md>
          x+(x+1)+(x+2)+(x+3)=134
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write an equation to represent the question, is there a set of three
          consecutive numbers that add up to 45?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write an expression that represents the product of two consecutive
          numbers.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write an expression that represents the sum of four consecutive odd
          numbers.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write an expression that represents the sum of a number and its
          reciprocal.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="exA-PP4">
    <title>Polygon Puzzle</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In a polygon puzzle your goal is to fill in the circles so that the sum
        of two circles connected by a line gives the value on the line.
      </p>

      <image source="SquarePuzzle.png" width="25%">
        <description>
          <p>
            A square puzzle with sides labeled 12, 14, 7, and 9.
          </p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a solution to the polygon puzzle given above.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is a second solution possible?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose we want to generalize our solutions.
          Express the upper left circle as <m>p</m> and then write the remaining
          circles in terms of <m>p</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="exa-PT">
    <title>Pascal's Triangle</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The image below shows a portion of Pascal's Triangle.
      </p>
        <image source="pascal.png" width="75%">
      <description>
        <p>Pascal's triangle with 6 completed rows and three blank rows</p>
      </description>
    </image>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Describe any patterns you see present in rows or diagonals of Pascal's triangle.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Fill in the missing values in the bottom rows of the triangle.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    
    
    
  </example>
</worksheet>